### What is the 4Sum problem?

The 4Sum problem asks you to find every unique group of four numbers (a quadruplet) in an array whose sum equals a given target. The result must contain no duplicate quadruplets, and each quadruplet must list its elements in non-decreasing order. This is a direct extension of the well-known 3Sum problem with one extra level of nesting.

In a banking context, the National Bank of India compliance team investigates cases where four transaction amounts together equal a suspicious target figure, indicating a single large payment split across four legs.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class FourSumFinder {

    public static List<List<Integer>> fourSum(int[] amounts, int target) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (amounts == null || amounts.length < 4) return result;
        Arrays.sort(amounts);
        int n = amounts.length;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 3; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && amounts[i] == amounts[i - 1]) continue; // skip duplicate first
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n - 2; j++) {
                if (j > i + 1 && amounts[j] == amounts[j - 1]) continue; // skip duplicate second
                int left = j + 1, right = n - 1;
                while (left < right) {
                    long sum = (long) amounts[i] + amounts[j] + amounts[left] + amounts[right];
                    if (sum == target) {
                        result.add(Arrays.asList(amounts[i], amounts[j], amounts[left], amounts[right]));
                        while (left < right && amounts[left] == amounts[left + 1]) left++;  // skip dup left
                        while (left < right && amounts[right] == amounts[right - 1]) right--; // skip dup right
                        left++;
                        right--;
                    } else if (sum < target) {
                        left++;
                    } else {
                        right--;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

### Why does 4Sum build on 3Sum?

The 3Sum pattern fixes one element, then uses a two-pointer scan for the remaining two. The 4Sum pattern adds one more fixed loop on top. By fixing two elements with nested loops, the remaining problem reduces to a two-sum search that the two-pointer technique handles in linear time. This layering is what gives the O(n^3) time complexity: two loops each cost O(n) and the inner two-pointer scan costs O(n).

```java
// Conceptual reduction:
// 4Sum(target) -> for each a: 3Sum(target - a)
// 3Sum(remainder) -> for each b: 2Sum(remainder - b)
// 2Sum is solved with two pointers on a sorted array
```

### How does the two-pointer scan work after fixing two elements?

After fixing the first element at index `i` and the second at index `j`, you set `left = j + 1` and `right = n - 1`. You compute the sum of all four values. If the sum equals the target, you record the quadruplet and move both pointers inward. If the sum is less than the target, you move the left pointer right to increase the sum. If the sum is greater, you move the right pointer left to decrease it.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

public class TwoPointerDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        int[] sorted = {-2, -1, 0, 0, 1, 2};
        int target = 0;
        // Fix i=0 (-2), j=1 (-1), need left+right = 3
        // left=2 (0), right=5 (2): sum = -2 + -1 + 0 + 2 = -1 < 0, move left
        // left=3 (0), right=5 (2): sum = -2 + -1 + 0 + 2 = -1 < 0, move left
        // left=4 (1), right=5 (2): sum = -2 + -1 + 1 + 2 = 0 == target, record!
        System.out.println("Found: [-2, -1, 1, 2]");
    }
}
```

### How do you handle duplicate quadruplets?

Duplicate skipping happens at all four selection levels. At the outer loop, if `amounts[i]` equals `amounts[i - 1]`, skip it. At the inner loop, if `amounts[j]` equals `amounts[j - 1]` within the current `i` iteration, skip it. After recording a quadruplet, advance `left` past all repeated values and retreat `right` past all repeated values. These four skips guarantee that every reported quadruplet is unique without storing results in a set.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

public class DuplicateSkipDemo {

    public static void showSkipping() {
        int[] amounts = {-2, -1, -1, 0, 0, 1, 2, 2};
        int target = 0;
        // i=0 (-2): j=1 (-1), j=2 (-1) is skipped because amounts[2]==amounts[1]
        // After finding a quad, left skips past repeated 0s, right skips past repeated 2s
        // This prevents [-2, -1, 0, 2] from appearing twice
        System.out.println("Duplicates handled at all four levels");
    }
}
```

### What about integer overflow?

When summing four values that are close to Integer.MAX_VALUE or Integer.MIN_VALUE, the sum can overflow a 32-bit int. You must cast to `long` before adding. In a banking context where amounts can be large, this is a real concern.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

public class OverflowDemo {

    public static void showOverflow() {
        int[] amounts = {1000000000, 1000000000, 1000000000, 1000000000};
        int target = -294967296; // would overflow with int sum

        // Wrong: int sum overflows
        int wrongSum = amounts[0] + amounts[1] + amounts[2] + amounts[3]; // overflows!
        System.out.println("Wrong sum (overflow): " + wrongSum);

        // Correct: cast to long
        long correctSum = (long) amounts[0] + amounts[1] + amounts[2] + amounts[3];
        System.out.println("Correct sum (long): " + correctSum);
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong sum (overflow): -294967296
Correct sum (long): 4000000000
```

### How does this generalize to k-sum?

The same template works for any k-sum. You recursively fix elements until the dimension reduces to 2, then apply the two-pointer base case. Each recursive fix adds one factor of n to the time complexity, giving O(n^(k-1)) total.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class KSumSolver {

    public static List<List<Integer>> kSum(int[] nums, long target, int k, int start) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (start >= nums.length) return result;

        if (k == 2) {
            int left = start, right = nums.length - 1;
            while (left < right) {
                long sum = (long) nums[left] + nums[right];
                if (sum == target) {
                    result.add(new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(nums[left], nums[right])));
                    while (left < right && nums[left] == nums[left + 1]) left++;
                    while (left < right && nums[right] == nums[right - 1]) right--;
                    left++; right--;
                } else if (sum < target) left++;
                else right--;
            }
        } else {
            for (int i = start; i < nums.length - k + 1; i++) {
                if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;
                for (List<Integer> sub : kSum(nums, target - nums[i], k - 1, i + 1)) {
                    sub.add(0, nums[i]);
                    result.add(sub);
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to skip duplicates at one of the four levels, which causes duplicate quadruplets in the output. Another mistake is not using `long` for the sum, which causes integer overflow for large inputs.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: missing duplicate skip at inner loop
    public static void wrongVersion(int[] nums, int target) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 3; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length - 2; j++) {
                // BUG: no duplicate skip for j!
                // This produces duplicate quadruplets
                // Fix: add "if (j > i + 1 && nums[j] == nums[j-1]) continue;"
            }
        }
    }

    // WRONG: using int for sum
    public static void wrongOverflow(int[] nums) {
        // BUG: int sum can overflow with 4 large values
        int sum = nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2] + nums[3];
        // Fix: long sum = (long) nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2] + nums[3];
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The time complexity is O(n^3). Sorting takes O(n log n). The two nested loops contribute O(n^2) and the two-pointer scan inside contributes O(n), giving O(n^3) total. The space complexity is O(1) extra beyond the result list, since sorting is in-place and no additional data structures are needed.

| Approach | Time | Space | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Brute force (4 nested loops) | O(n^4) | O(1) | Too slow for large inputs |
| Sort + two fix loops + two pointers | O(n^3) | O(1) | Optimal for 4Sum |
| Hash map for inner pair | O(n^3) worst case | O(n^2) | Harder duplicate handling |
| Generalized k-sum recursive | O(n^(k-1)) | O(k) recursion | Flexible for any k |

### What about early termination optimizations?

You can prune the search by checking if the smallest possible sum (using the four smallest remaining elements) exceeds the target, or if the largest possible sum (using the four largest) is less than the target. These checks let you skip entire iterations of the outer loops when no valid quadruplet can exist in that range.

```java
package com.bank.coding;

public class EarlyTermination {

    public static void optimizedOuterLoop(int[] nums, int target) {
        int n = nums.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 3; i++) {
            // Prune: smallest possible sum with current i
            long minSum = (long) nums[i] + nums[i + 1] + nums[i + 2] + nums[i + 3];
            if (minSum > target) break; // all future i values will be even larger

            // Prune: largest possible sum with current i
            long maxSum = (long) nums[i] + nums[n - 3] + nums[n - 2] + nums[n - 1];
            if (maxSum < target) continue; // this i is too small, try next

            // ... proceed with inner loop and two pointers
        }
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask how you would handle the problem if duplicates were allowed in the output, how you would extend to 5Sum or general k-sum, and whether a hash-based approach would be better. They may also ask about the tradeoff between the sorting-based approach and a set-based deduplication approach. The sorting approach is preferred because it handles duplicates naturally and uses less space.

```java
// Follow-up: if duplicates are allowed, simply remove the skip logic
// Follow-up: for k-sum, use the recursive kSum method shown above
// Follow-up: hash approach stores pairs in a map, but duplicate handling is complex
```
